<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-undetermined-guess-practice" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Practice: Finding the Right Guess for <m>y_p</m></title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO PROSE: REMIND OF THE TWO CASES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Before we put together a full general solution, let's make sure we're
    comfortable identifying the correct guess for <m>y_p</m>. We have two
    cases to keep in mind, summarized below.
  </p>

  <assemblage>
    <title>Choosing the Guess for <m>y_p</m></title>
    <p>
      Given <m>ay'' + by' + cy = m_1\cos(kt) + m_2\sin(kt)</m>:
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Normal case</alert> (from <xref ref="obs-undetermined-strategy"/>):
          use the guess <m>y_p = A\cos(kt) + B\sin(kt)</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Case of duplication</alert> (from <xref ref="thm-case-of-duplication"/>):
          if the ODE can be written as <m>y'' + k^2 y = m_1\cos(kt) + m_2\sin(kt)</m>
          (same <m>k</m> on both sides, no <m>y'</m> term), use the modified guess
          <m>y_p = t\bigl(A\cos(kt) + B\sin(kt)\bigr)</m>.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
      The key step in identifying the case of duplication: write the ODE in
      standard form (divide through so the leading coefficient is <m>1</m>) and
      check whether the coefficient of <m>y</m> equals <m>k^2</m>.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    The questions below give you practice at just this identification step; you
    don't need to solve for <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, just state the correct form of
    the guess.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY: 4 CHECKPOINT QUESTIONS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <activity xml:id="activity-guess-practice">
    <title>Identifying the Correct Guess</title>

    <!-- ── Q1: Standard normal case ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-guess-practice-normal">
      <title>A standard example</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the correct form of the guess <m>y_p</m> for finding a
          particular solution to
          <me>y'' + 3y' - 10y = 4\cos(6t) - 7\sin(6t)?</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(6t) + B\sin(6t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Both terms on the right-hand side share frequency <m>k = 6</m>,
              so the guess uses <m>\cos(6t)</m> and <m>\sin(6t)</m>. The coefficient
              of <m>y</m> is <m>-10 \neq 36 = k^2</m>, so this is the normal case,
              no extra <m>t</m> needed. <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(6t) - B\sin(6t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The minus sign on the right-hand side doesn't change the form of
              the guess. We always write <m>y_p = A\cos(6t) + B\sin(6t)</m> and
              let the algebra determine the signs of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> when
              we solve; one of them may turn out to be negative.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = t\bigl(A\cos(6t) + B\sin(6t)\bigr)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This is the case-of-duplication guess, but we're not in that case here.
              Duplication occurs only when the ODE has the form <m>y'' + k^2 y = \ldots</m>
              with the same <m>k</m> on both sides. Here the coefficient of <m>y</m>
              is <m>-10 \neq 36 = k^2</m>, so we don't need the extra <m>t</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(6t) + B\sin(6t) + C\cos(6t) + D\sin(6t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Since both terms on the right have the same frequency, we only need
              one pair of undetermined coefficients. The guess <m>A\cos(6t) + B\sin(6t)</m>
              already has enough flexibility to match both the cosine and sine parts
              of the right-hand side simultaneously.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- ── Q2: Easy duplication case ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-guess-practice-dup-easy">
      <title>A case of duplication</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the correct form of the guess <m>y_p</m> for finding a
          particular solution to
          <me>y'' + 25y = 3\cos(5t) + 8\sin(5t)?</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>y_p = t\bigl(A\cos(5t) + B\sin(5t)\bigr)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Both terms on the right-hand side have frequency <m>k = 5</m>,
              and the coefficient of <m>y</m> is <m>25 = 5^2 = k^2</m>. This is the
              case of duplication, so we multiply the usual guess by <m>t</m>.
              <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(5t) + B\sin(5t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This is the usual guess, but it will fail here. The ODE
              <m>y'' + 25y = 0</m> has characteristic equation
              <m>r^2 + 25 = 0</m>, giving roots <m>r = \pm 5i</m>, so the
              homogeneous solution is <m>c_1\cos(5t) + c_2\sin(5t)</m>. Our
              guess is already a solution to the homogeneous equation, so plugging
              it in gives <m>0</m> on the left, never matching the right-hand
              side.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(5t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Even setting aside the duplication issue, a cosine-only guess
              won't work for a right-hand side that includes <m>\sin(5t)</m>.
              And since we're in the case of duplication, we need the
              <m>t</m>-multiplied form as well.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = t\cdot A\cos(5t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              We're in the case of duplication, so we do need the extra
              <m>t</m>, but the full guess must include both <m>\cos(5t)</m>
              and <m>\sin(5t)</m> inside, not just the cosine.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- ── Q3: Negative coefficient trap (NOT duplication) ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-guess-practice-negative">
      <title>Watch out for a negative coefficient</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the correct form of the guess <m>y_p</m> for finding a
          particular solution to
          <me>y'' - 9y = 5\cos(3t) - 2\sin(3t)?</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Even though <m>k = 3</m> and <m>3^2 = 9</m>, the
              coefficient of <m>y</m> is <m>-9</m>, not <m>+9</m>. The case
              of duplication requires the coefficient of <m>y</m> to be
              <m>+k^2</m>, which means the homogeneous solutions are
              <m>\cos(kt)</m> and <m>\sin(kt)</m>. Here the characteristic
              equation is <m>r^2 - 9 = 0</m>, giving real roots <m>r = \pm 3</m>
              and homogeneous solution <m>c_1 e^{3t} + c_2 e^{-3t}</m>, with no
              trig at all! So the usual guess works fine, with both terms
              covered by a single pair <m>A, B</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = t\bigl(A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)\bigr)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              It's tempting to see <m>k = 3</m> and the number <m>9 = 3^2</m>
              and call this duplication, but the sign matters! The coefficient
              of <m>y</m> is <m>-9</m>, not <m>+9</m>. The homogeneous equation
              <m>y'' - 9y = 0</m> has real exponential solutions
              <m>e^{3t}</m> and <m>e^{-3t}</m>, not trig functions. The usual
              guess is not a homogeneous solution, so the extra <m>t</m> is not
              needed.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = Ae^{3t} + Be^{-3t}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              These are the homogeneous solutions, not a form that can match
              <m>5\cos(3t) - 2\sin(3t)</m> on the right-hand side. The guess should mirror
              the form of <m>g(t)</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(3t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The right-hand side has both a cosine and a sine term, so a
              cosine-only guess can't possibly match both. We always need
              <m>y_p = A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)</m> when <m>g(t)</m> involves
              trig at frequency <m>k</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- ── Q4: Divide through first ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-guess-practice-divide-first">
      <title>Remember to put the ODE in standard form first</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the correct form of the guess <m>y_p</m> for finding a
          particular solution to
          <me>2y'' + 18y = \cos(3t) + 4\sin(3t)?</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>y_p = t\bigl(A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)\bigr)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! The trick here is to put the ODE in standard form first
              by dividing through by <m>2</m>:
              <me>y'' + 9y = \tfrac{1}{2}\cos(3t) + 2\sin(3t).</me>
              Now the coefficient of <m>y</m> is <m>9 = 3^2 = k^2</m>, so we
              are in the case of duplication. The modified guess is
              <m>y_p = t(A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t))</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This looks right at first glance, but don't forget to put the
              ODE in standard form first. Dividing by <m>2</m> gives
              <m>y'' + 9y = \frac{1}{2}\cos(3t) + 2\sin(3t)</m>, and now
              <m>9 = 3^2 = k^2</m>, so this is the case of duplication.
              The usual guess will give <m>0</m> on the left-hand side.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = t\bigl(A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t)\bigr)</m>, but first replace the right-hand side with <m>\cos(3t) + 4\sin(3t)</m> since we'll divide by 2 later.</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Dividing by <m>2</m> changes the right-hand side too, giving
              <m>\tfrac{1}{2}\cos(3t) + 2\sin(3t)</m>, not the original. But
              the form of the guess is otherwise correct:
              <m>y_p = t(A\cos(3t) + B\sin(3t))</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = t\bigl(A\cos(18t) + B\sin(18t)\bigr)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The frequency in the guess always comes from the right-hand side
              of the ODE. Both <m>\cos(3t)</m> and <m>\sin(3t)</m> on the right
              have frequency <m>k = 3</m>. The coefficient <m>18</m> is from the
              left-hand side and doesn't set the frequency.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- ── Q5: Near-duplication trap ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-guess-practice-near-dup">
      <title>A subtle one</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the correct form of the guess <m>y_p</m> for finding a
          particular solution to
          <me>y'' + 4y = \cos(4t) + 3\sin(4t)?</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(4t) + B\sin(4t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! It's tempting to see the <m>4</m> in <m>y'' + 4y</m> and
              the <m>4t</m> on the right and call this duplication, but check
              carefully. The frequency is <m>k = 4</m>, so we need the coefficient
              of <m>y</m> to equal <m>k^2 = 16</m> for duplication. Here the
              coefficient is only <m>4 \neq 16</m>, so this is the normal case.
              <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = t\bigl(A\cos(4t) + B\sin(4t)\bigr)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This is the most natural wrong answer here! The <m>4</m> in <m>y'' + 4y</m>
              and the <m>4t</m> on the right look like they match, but duplication requires
              the coefficient of <m>y</m> to equal <m>k^2</m>, not <m>k</m>.
              Since <m>k = 4</m>, we need the coefficient to be <m>16</m>, not <m>4</m>.
              The homogeneous equation <m>y'' + 4y = 0</m> has solutions
              <m>\cos(2t)</m> and <m>\sin(2t)</m> (frequency <m>2</m>, not <m>4</m>),
              so the guess doesn't duplicate anything.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(2t) + B\sin(2t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The frequency in the guess must match the right-hand side, which
              has frequency <m>k = 4</m>. The <m>2</m> here comes from solving
              the homogeneous equation <m>y'' + 4y = 0</m> (whose solutions
              oscillate at frequency <m>\sqrt{4} = 2</m>), but that's not where
              the guess frequency comes from.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = t\bigl(A\cos(2t) + B\sin(2t)\bigr)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Neither the frequency nor the duplication call is right here.
              The guess frequency should match the right-hand side (<m>k = 4</m>),
              and since the coefficient of <m>y</m> is <m>4 \neq 16 = k^2</m>,
              we're not in the case of duplication and don't need the extra <m>t</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       BRIDGE TO NEXT PAGE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Now that we can reliably identify the right guess, the next step is to use
    it to build the full general solution: not just a particular solution, but
    the complete picture including the complementary function.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
